  "lesson-roblox-7-1": {
    "lessonId": "lesson-roblox-7-1",
    "moduleId": "module-07",
    "order": "1",
    "title": "7.1 - Two Worlds: Client and Server",
    "learningObjectives": [
      "Explain what runs on the client vs the server",
      "Identify trust boundaries in a multiplayer game",
      "Decide where gameplay logic should live for security and fairness",
      "Build a tiny client-server ping demo"
    ],
    "estimatedTime": 60,
    "theory": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Two computers, one game",
          "content": "In Roblox, your game is split into two worlds:\n- **Client**: each player's own app (camera, input, UI)\n- **Server**: the shared authority (real game state)\n\nIf you put important logic only on the client, exploiters can fake it. If you put everything on the server, the game feels laggy. Great devs choose the right side for each job."
        },
        {
          "title": "What belongs where",
          "content": "Use this rule of thumb:\n- **Client**: visuals, button clicks, local effects\n- **Server**: currency, inventory, damage, quest progress\n\nClient asks. Server decides.\n\n```lua\n-- LocalScript\nprint(\"I can read input and update UI\")\n\n-- Script (ServerScriptService)\nprint(\"I validate and save shared data\")\n```"
        },
        {
          "title": "First handshake",
          "content": "Set up a tiny communication test before building big systems.\n\n```lua\n-- LocalScript\nlocal ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(\"ReplicatedStorage\")\nlocal Ping = ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(\"PingServer\")\nPing:FireServer(\"Hello from client!\")\n\n-- Script\nPing.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player, msg)\n    print(player.Name .. \" says: \" .. msg)\nend)\n```"
        }
      ]
    },
    "practiceTask": {
      "title": "Practice: Trust Boundary Map",
      "difficulty": "beginner",
      "description": "Create a one-page map for your game idea: list 10 actions and label each as Client, Server, or Both. Then build the ping demo and confirm messages appear in Output from real players.",
      "hints": [
        "If cheating would hurt balance, that logic must run on server.",
        "Use ReplicatedStorage for shared remotes, not ServerStorage.",
        "Name remotes clearly: RequestPurchase, CompleteQuest, EquipItem."
      ],
      "optionalChallenge": "Add a second argument to your ping message with `os.clock()` and display round-trip timing in the client UI."
    }
  },
  "lesson-roblox-7-2": {
    "lessonId": "lesson-roblox-7-2",
    "moduleId": "module-07",
    "order": "2",
    "title": "7.2 - RemoteEvent",
    "learningObjectives": [
      "Use `RemoteEvent` to send one-way messages",
      "Handle `OnServerEvent` and `OnClientEvent` safely",
      "Pass structured data in event payloads",
      "Guard against invalid or spammed requests"
    ],
    "estimatedTime": 60,
    "theory": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "RemoteEvent in plain English",
          "content": "RemoteEvent is for one-way communication:\n- Client -> Server: FireServer(...)\n- Server -> Client: FireClient(player, ...) or FireAllClients(...)\n\nThink of it as game mail: no direct return value, just a message sent now."
        },
        {
          "title": "Minimal event pattern",
          "content": "Always validate what arrives from client.\n\n```lua\n-- Server\nBuyItemEvent.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player, itemId)\n    if type(itemId) ~= \"string\" then return end\n    print(player.Name, \"requested\", itemId)\nend)\n```\n\nNever assume the client sent honest data."
        },
        {
          "title": "Sending updates back",
          "content": "After server processes a request, notify UI with a result event.\n\n```lua\n-- Server\nPurchaseResult:FireClient(player, true, \"Sword purchased!\")\n\n-- Client\nPurchaseResult.OnClientEvent:Connect(function(ok, message)\n    StatusLabel.Text = message\nend)\n```"
        }
      ]
    },
    "practiceTask": {
      "title": "Practice: Event Message Bus",
      "difficulty": "beginner",
      "description": "Build two remotes: `RequestAction` (client->server) and `ActionResult` (server->client). Send an item id, validate type on server, then return success/fail text to UI.",
      "hints": [
        "Use simple payloads first: string id, number price, boolean result.",
        "Return early on invalid data to avoid deep nested code.",
        "Log suspicious payloads for debugging and anti-cheat."
      ],
      "optionalChallenge": "Add a 0.3s cooldown table per player to ignore rapid spam requests."
    }
  },
